The {{nihongo|'''Jutsu Restoration'''|樹唾維新|Jutsu Ishin}}, also known as the Heisei Restoration, was a defining period of political and constitutional upheaval in [[Okaiken]] that lasted from 2011 to 2016. It marked the end of the "Hollow Center" era established by the 1970 [[Quiet Revolution]] and the return of the [[Emperor of Okaiken]] to the position of Head of State.

The era is characterized by its dramatic shift from the paralysis of the "Lost Decades" to an aggressive, nationalist technocracy. It began with the peaceful, popular reinstatement of Emperor [[Masahito]] following the government shutdown of 2011, but evolved into a volatile period of radical modernization under the 27-year-old Prime Minister [[Toshiro Kurosawa]]. The Restoration formally concluded with Kurosawa's resignation in 2016, which solidified the modern "Dual Sovereignty" political system.

==Background==
===The Winter of Stagnation===
By late 2010, the political system established by the 1970 Constitution, which had stripped the Emperor of all executive authority, had reached a breaking point. The government, led by Prime Minister [[Heitarō Seda]], was paralyzed by a "Twisted Diet," where the opposition controlled the Upper House and refused to pass the budget.

Following Seda's resignation due to health reasons in January 2011, his successor [[Tadahisa Jōhei]] inherited a broken state. The "120-Day Shutdown" ensued, where government services ceased, garbage piled up in the capital, and regional power grids failed due to a lack of maintenance funds. The public, furious at the "do-nothing democracy," began calling for a hard reset of the state.

==Phase I: The Imperial Return (2011–2012)==
===The Emperor's Intervention===
[[File:Emperor Masahito TV Broadcast.png|thumb|left|250px|Emperor Masahito delivers a televised speech announcing the restoration of of the Imperial Family]]
On April 20, 2011, [[Emperor Masahito]] broke four decades of imperial silence. In a televised address that stunned the nation, he publicly chastised the Jōhei cabinet for their incompetence, famously stating that if they could not authorize the funds to heat the homes of his people, they had forfeited their right to rule them.

This intervention catalyzed a massive public movement. Riding this wave, Prime Minister Jōhei passed a series of emergency referendums in late 2011. The Capital Restoration Act reverted the capital's name from "Central Metropolitan District" back to its historic name, [[Okami]]. The Sovereignty Act formally designated the Emperor as the Head of State with specific reserve powers to resolve parliamentary deadlocks.

===The Yanbaru Scandal===
The euphoria of the restoration was short-lived. In March 2012, the Yanbaru Earthquake struck the southern coast. While the quake was moderate, the damage was catastrophic due to corruption. It was revealed that the Jōhei administration had allowed billions of yen in "Resilience Funding" to be siphoned off by construction cartels, resulting in sea walls that crumbled instantly. Jōhei's approval ratings evaporated, and he resigned in disgrace on August 10, 2012, handing power to veteran politician [[Sasuke Genba]].

==Phase II: The Vacuum (2012–2013)==
Sasuke Genba’s premiership was tragic and brief. Tasked with cleaning up the LDP’s corruption while managing a resurgent Emperor who was now actively auditing government documents, Genba crumbled under the pressure. On February 15, 2013, faced with a collapsing economy and a vote of no confidence he could not survive, Genba committed suicide in the Prime Minister's Official Residence.

==Phase III: The Kurosawa Doctrine (2013–2016)==
===The Rise of the Young Shogun===
In the power vacuum left by Genba’s death, the LDP elected [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], the 27-year-old Minister of Infrastructure, as Party President. Kurosawa, a protégé of Emperor Masahito, campaigned on a platform of radical modernization. He won the 2013 snap election, becoming the youngest democratically elected world leader in history.

===The Concrete to Code Shift===
Kurosawa’s administration implemented the "Kurosawa Doctrine," a policy mix of aggressive nationalism and digital statism. His economic pivot diverted funds from traditional construction, which was riddled with corruption, to the [[Yagikoshu Data Corridor]], a massive state-backed AI and server infrastructure project. In foreign policy, arguing that international organizations stifled Sanese sovereignty, Kurosawa withdrew Okaiken from the [[United Sovereign Nations]] (USNW) in 2014. He re-adopted the [[Sanese En|Sanese En (¥)]] as the sole legal currency, decoupling the nation from global monetary policy. Furthermore, his government integrated the [[Satori]] AI system into the bureaucracy, aiming to replace corrupt human civil servants with algorithmic governance.

==Collapse and Legacy==
===Data-Gate and the Fall===
The Restoration era came to an abrupt end in June 2016. Leaked documents revealed that Kurosawa’s "Satori" system was being used not just for traffic management, but for unauthorized surveillance of opposition politicians and journalists. This scandal, known as "Data-Gate," shattered the public's trust in the "Technocratic Dream." 

Facing pressures and a recall LDP leadership race spearheaded by [[Sadakazu Tanizaki]], Kurosawa resigned on June 3, 2016. In the subsequent election, the LDP suffered a historic defeat to the [[Communist Party of Okaiken|Communist Party]] under [[Sho Aoyagi]], who promised a return to human-centric governance.

===The Iron Survivor===
Despite the collapse of the Kurosawa government, the Imperial Restoration itself survived. [[Emperor Masahito]] successfully distanced himself from the scandal, framing his role as the "Auditor" who had allowed the exposure of the corruption. The Jutsu Restoration is thus remembered as a successful correction of the state, establishing the current political equilibrium where the Emperor acts as the ultimate check on civilian power.

